// pages/backdrop.plm — the wallpaper: posterised, dithered, run through the tube.
//
// Drawn inside the scene's surface, below everything else. One group shader
// carries both QML passes (shaders/paper.wgsl): the picture is pushed to four
// levels through an ordered Bayer dither, then the tube's barrel curve,
// chromatic aberration, bloom, scanlines and vignette are applied over it. The
// shader draws the legacy dot-grid fallback itself when no wallpaper path was
// published, so the scene needs no second shader for that case.
//
// The picture is drawn at the whole surface and `fit: cover`: it keeps its own
// proportions and the screen crops it, which is the reference's
// `Image.PreserveAspectCrop` (background/BackgroundImage.qml:300-310). The cell it
// comes from is declared with the picture's own shape (`image wall = from wallpaper,
// 0, 0`), so nothing letterboxes it on the way to the atlas — a fixed 16:9 cell put
// black bands over a panorama, and the login screen showed a strip between them.
// `screen.*` inside the surface belongs to its own copy (INTERFACES.md §3.2), so the
// arithmetic lives here and not in a `let` the copies would share.

part Backdrop {

    group deck {
        // barrel, aberration, scanlines, grille, bloom, vignette, dither
        // amount, dither cell (paper.wgsl)
        shader: paper, 0.008, 0.6, 0.13, 0.05, 0.35, 0.35, 0.55, 2

        image wall {
            at: 0, 0
            size: screen.width, screen.height
            fit: cover
            show: wall_ok
        }
    }

    // The scrim of LockContent.qml: 18 % of the theme's darkest tone over the tube, so the eye
    // lands on the clock and the card. It is `skin.coal` — the wallpaper's colors.background
    // role, the same tone the drawers and the launcher lay under themselves — so a light wallpaper
    // veils with its own light tone instead of frozen black. The vignette lives in paper.wgsl.
    //
    // It covers three screens' worth, centred on the scene's origin, because `size:` on a
    // `kind: lock` surface is "the box that gets centred on each monitor" and the runtime's own
    // reference says to paint the backdrop large, "what lies around it shows too" (pleamar --docs
    // reference, the lock-screen section; its example draws its backdrop from -4000). A box
    // written `full` is the monitor itself (`pleamar-lock-full.patch`: a zero box was centred on
    // the monitor, which put the scene's origin at the monitor's centre and painted the whole
    // login screen half a panel away — the card in a corner, the rest black, no error anywhere),
    // so with one monitor this is a plain full-screen pane; with several it is what keeps every
    // face covered, since the scene sees the largest one and each face shows its own part.
    box scrim {
        from: -screen.width, -screen.height
        size: screen.width * 3, screen.height * 3
        color: skin.coal
        opacity: 18%
    }
}
